Z||000006||Indy-2M4||12-28-2001||05:42 AM||||I've never had any display problems, however the stock Delco radio in both my '84SE and '87GT have a tendancy to zip off channel all by themselves. The display will still display the frequency set, but it sound as if someone has grabbed hold of the tuner and and going through the band.<P>------------------<BR>Kathy<BR>http:\\sofa.iwarp.com<BR>    <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/IndyCrueSig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Eric_PA||12-28-2001||06:28 AM||||I have had that trouble with all three of my pontiacs that came stock with the Delco CD player. The screen doesn't fully light up and reacts slow until warmed up. Another problem I had with two of them was that the volume knob starts to act up after a while. I'm just glad those cars had steering wheel buttons or it would have drove me crazy. Other than that I think they are great stereos.<P>Eric<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||12-28-2001||07:25 AM||||the one in my Ferraro only worked at all once in a while. The Bose in the vette has no radio, no display but tape deck works??<P>as for digital display, the heater in the vette is climate control, all the lites and numbers flicker and blink till it gets warm inside. But it does work fine. maybe leds dont like cold??<P>my opinion in general is GM needs to stay out of radio business, lol||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnF||12-28-2001||08:16 AM||||A person who owns and operates a radio replacement shop told me that the older GM radios (like the ones in my Fieros) were prone to the motherboard actually melting and running components together in odd spots to give wierd and unfixable responses.<P><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://photos.msn.com/ImageServer/Image.aspx?Image=0h9xCkj4Yyo6VbTdcNrmhglJSkAWB6C91volMeDlp0h*oii8TPGa20kyOxpdcqzP8qgFSFmsURSXXLzrLUqyll5ofimdP92*FNFuzBBDM1PEajQxQ*Q8WWq*rfvz6!x4pUtE2EsH24ClDyP3oWXL*H41TkaSbtSsDJGlSZ4dol6UKjqoNXe9R!uqr1PsTEkyvCkblQOE!Ntm6y7ZIR0Yh4dj5E3iRUs!aOGI2F9kotBxoa!4otRtSuGucZ1Pen9q24d0hrglVvzK*EY4KHAosZ4!AMC1kyuNFUR*urYcA*t61aKP0zREsXUVoyq0!cXPxGXJFnyglKwpMClTPrmLCQv*qNBY9KpHMNz9*AOylphEbbQcqGuVPQ$$"> <P>You can have all the SPEED you can afford!||||reg||10
Z||000010||Raydar||12-28-2001||12:09 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indy-2M4:<BR><B>...have a tendancy to zip off channel all by themselves. The display will still display the frequency set, but it sound as if someone has grabbed hold of the tuner and and going through the band.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>I have fixed this problem in a radio that I bought secondhand. Mine was the "no knobs, all buttons" model, but the one with knobs and equalizer uses most of the same guts.<BR> <BR>It's caused by a broken solder connection on the tuner board. (VCO steering line, to be exact.) <P>I have diagrams of that board. I can scan and post a pic of where I had to resolder the connection. If you are handy with a soldering iron, it should be an easy fix. At least mine was. I got lucky. YMMV.<P>Not sure about the displays.<P>Roger, if your tape works, but the radio and clock don't, I'd look for a blown fuse or a disconnected wire. <BR>Not too sure about the Vette radio, but if the continuous 12V source is missing from the Fiero radio, it does the same thing. It's the little plug with two wires, on a clip on the back of the radio. One is constant 12V, the other is the display dimmer.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||12-28-2001||02:38 PM||||But if power was disconnected, or fuse blown how would the tape player still work? I was thinking of putting a $100 RS cheapo in this weekend. but ill check the power wires when I take it out first, before the RS trip.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||12-28-2001||07:20 PM||||If it's like the Fiero radio, there is a switched 12V wire in the 12 pin plug (or three 4 pin plugs). This is where the amplifier, tape deck, and <I>most of</I> the rest of the radio gets its power. <BR>There is a 12V battery wire in the separate 2 wire connector. This is a continuous 12V that holds the station presets and the clock memory. It seems like if that 12V is missing, the tuner doesn't work. <BR>I could be wrong, but it's worth a try.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||88CoupeV6||12-28-2001||11:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B>A person who owns and operates a radio replacement shop told me that the older GM radios (like the ones in my Fieros) were prone to the motherboard actually melting and running components together in odd spots to give wierd and unfixable responses.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I think that is what is going on with the radio in my 88GT. The display is dead, but it lights every now & then. It also lights all up so everything on the bisplay screen is on at once and the sound is only working on 1 side of the speakers.  Time to get a new one  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88coupeV6.jpg">       <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>    <A HREF="http://www.fieropride.com/nefa.htm" TARGET=_blank>Join New England Fiero Association</A>||||reg||1
Z||000014||rogergarrison||12-29-2001||07:17 AM||||tks raydar, Im going to tear into it this weekend. Nothing else to do - cold n snow here all weekend and believe it or not, theres not a single thing to do to the Ferraro!<BR>(knocks on wood)||||reg||1
